19. But agency must be constrained


This is where your Risk Firewall becomes the most important component.


The neural network proposes:



a_t



The firewall evaluates:



R(a_t,s_t)



and returns:



a_t^*



where:



a_t^*=
\begin{cases}
a_t & \text{if safe}\\
Modified(a_t) & \text{if partially safe}\\
HOLD & \text{if unsafe}\\
SELL & \text{if emergency}
\end{cases}



The model therefore doesn't possess unrestricted authority.



20. The Risk Firewall


It can enforce hard rules like:


Maximum position



PositionValue\leq0.5B



where  is agent budget.


Maximum trade risk



Risk_{trade}\leq0.01B



Maximum drawdown



DD>-10\%



If:



DD\leq-10\%



the agent is halted or de-risked.


Daily loss limit


For example:



Loss_{day}\leq2\%



Liquidity constraint


Don't permit an order larger than some fraction of expected available liquidity.


Correlation constraint


Don't allow the portfolio to accidentally become one giant technology-sector bet.


Emergency kill switch


If something goes catastrophically wrong:


ALL AGENTS → HALT




