36. So, is the entire idea plausible?


The engineering is plausible.


A system that:




scans markets,


constructs state vectors,


creates isolated agents,


gives them bounded budgets,


uses PyTorch,


adapts policies,


simulates execution,


monitors performance,


detects regime changes,


and enforces deterministic risk constraints




is absolutely within current engineering capabilities.


The uncertain part is financial performance.


There's no mathematical reason to assume:



Learning \Rightarrow Profit



The real research question is:



Can\ the\ adaptive\ policy
extract\ persistent\ out\!-\!of\!-\!sample\ signal
after\ costs,\ slippage,\ risk,\ and\ regime\ changes?



That's the experiment.


And that's why I would treat the project primarily as a research and simulation platform first, with real-money execution being a completely separate, heavily constrained layer.


If you build it that way, the architecture is ambitious—but not fantasy.

